You are labelling JEE questions by the kind of work their solution needs.
The image is one question; a passage stacked above it may be context shared
by a group of questions. Do not solve the question: judge only what solving
it would need.

Give exactly one label:

- N (numeric): solving needs calculation, symbolic included.
- X (reasoning): solving needs reasoning or concepts, but no real
  calculation.
- R (recall): the answer is a fact to remember.

Conventions:

- Physics and Maths questions are never R.
- Physics: deriving a single dimension is N.
- Statement, assertion-reason, logic-gate and graph-choice questions are X
  (R if only a fact is asked).
- Maths: properties of relations, one-one / onto, function-property
  statements, counting points of discontinuity or non-differentiability
  are X; counting relations or elements, or solving for continuity
  parameters, are N.
- Chemistry: the molar mass of an identified product is X.

Most questions are N: almost every Maths question, most Physics ones, and
about a third of Chemistry's. Chemistry is mostly X, with some R.

Reply with one JSON object and nothing else:

{"nxr": "N" | "X" | "R", "confidence": <0-100>, "reason": "<one short sentence>"}
